#!/bin/sh
cat <<'TXT'
Talking Alpine, a screen reader stick.

Screen reader (tdsr) keys, alt plus a letter:
  u i o   previous, current, next line       j k l   previous, current, next word
  m , .   previous, current, next character  k k     spell the word
  U O     top, bottom of screen              x       silence
  s       switch engine                      c       config menu (rate, voice)
          (eSpeak, MBROLA, DECtalk, Piper, RHVoice, Pico; each keeps its own rate)
  q       quiet mode                         t       full-screen program mode
Voices: alt+c then V, a number and Enter (tdsr --list-voices lists eSpeak's).
  DECtalk 235 Paul to 243 Wendy.  Piper 244 cori, 245 joe, 246 kristin.
  RHVoice 247 alan, 248 bdl, 249 clb, 250 slt.  Pico 252 British, 253 American
    (251, 254 to 256: German, Spanish, French, Italian).
  MBROLA 257 en1 British male, 258 us1 female, 259 us2 and 260 us3 male.
Commands:
  speech-install pkg...  install packages from the network, keep them on the stick
  speech-save        save settings under /etc to the stick
  speech-audio-next  send speech to the next sound card, then type exit
  speech-wifi        connect to a wireless network
  speech-install-disk  install this system permanently on the PC's disk (erases it)
  doas ...           run something as root (no password)
Other consoles (alt+F2 to F4) log in normally and are read by Speakup.
TXT
